---
description: Coordination rules for explicitly requested multi-agent iOS work.
alwaysApply: true
---

Apply this workflow when the user asks for orchestration or multiple agents. A simple edit does not require a multi-agent run.

1. Read applicable repository instructions, relevant project memory and its evidence, and docs/agent-task.md. Resolve the actual repository, workspace or project, scheme, and acceptance criteria before coding. Use grill-me in the interactive coordinator when product decisions need clarification, then to-milestones to write the task contract and milestone configuration.
2. Assign one small milestone to ios-implementer. Only one agent writes to this checkout at a time.
3. Once the writer finishes, run the project's approved build/test gate serially. For this starter, use bash tools/ios-harness/check.sh after its configuration is completed. Use XcodeBuildMCP, as bound in xcode-build-run.mdc, to run the app, capture screenshots for visual acceptance, and debug between gates, never during one.
4. Freeze edits while ios-correctness-reviewer and ios-ui-reviewer review in parallel. Give each the complete task, paths, implementation report, and gate evidence; they start without your conversation history.
5. Keep every result paired with its reviewer name. A missing, failed, cancelled, stopped, partial, or malformed mandatory result blocks the milestone.
6. Send real findings to one repair writer, who follows diagnosing-bugs for gate failures. Run the gate and both reviews again after every repair, including the final allowed repair. Allow at most two repair rounds.
7. Continue to the next milestone only when the gate passes and both mandatory reviews accept the current code. Report remaining findings when the repair limit is reached.
8. Do not commit, push, merge, release, change credentials, weaken tests, or edit acceptance criteria as part of this workflow.
9. Use isolated worktrees and explicit integration ownership before allowing concurrent writers. Unique DerivedData paths do not themselves prevent simulator conflicts.
10. After acceptance, the coordinator records durable codebase, business-logic, and dependency findings through the project-memory workflow, using domain-modeling for terms and hard-to-reverse decisions. Treat worker reports as proposals to verify, not facts to persist automatically.

These are agent instructions. The deterministic runner in tools/orchestrator owns scheduling when you invoke it; do not run an additional native editing agent beside that runner.
